+# Local MAPF Negotiation Core Design
+
+## Purpose
+
+This branch evaluates whether a bounded, core-only joint planner for at most
+four participants can avoid the combinatorial growth observed in RMF's current
+table/proposal/rollout negotiation. It is a feasibility experiment, not a
+production activation point. `CentralizedNegotiation::solve()` is used only as
+an opt-in A/B harness and fallback boundary.
+
+The existing holding-point alternative preservation at baseline `ccdb765`
+remains intact. No fleet adapter, `rmf_ros2`, external arbiter, corridor
+manager, corridor state machine, or robot deployment is in scope.
+
+## Existing Behavior and Failure Mode
+
+`CentralizedNegotiation` currently creates one `SimpleNegotiator` per agent and
+drives the same distributed negotiation protocol in process. A submission
+creates response tables for the remaining participants, so complete table
+orders grow factorially. Rejections add rollout alternative sets; a responder
+then explores combinations across rejecters. A per-rollout alternative cap does
+not bound aggregate table and Cartesian-product growth. This explains why the
+generic dynamic opposite-direction three-agent and 2-vs-2 cases can time out
+even when useful holding alternatives are present.
+
+`CentralizedNegotiation` already owns every required low-level input (start,
+goal, participant ID, planner, schedule viewer), while `schedule::Negotiation`
+does not own participant planners or goals. Therefore the former is the least
+invasive feasibility integration point. The production call boundary will be
+re-evaluated after the PoC against the real call graph; this design does not
+assume that production must integrate there or that adapter changes are
+inevitable.
+
+## Public Core Component
+
+Add independent `rmf_traffic::agv::LocalConflictResolver` API with:
+
+- `Agent`: participant ID, starts, goal, and shared `Planner`.
+- `Options`: configurable maximum agents (hard upper bound four), high-level
+  node budget, low-level replan budget, and wall-clock budget.
+- `Metrics`: status/termination reason, solve time, expanded high-level nodes,
+  low-level replans, conflicts examined/resolved, holding-point use, makespan,
+  total arrival time, and total delay.
+- `Result`: optional `unordered_map<ParticipantId, Plan>`, blocker IDs, and
+  metrics.
+
+The successful proposal uses ordinary RMF `Plan` objects. The API contains no
+map-specific waypoint, corridor identifier, robot name, compass direction, or
+fixed participant-count logic.
+
+## Bounded Prioritized Joint Search
+
+This first implementation is deliberately incomplete MAPF:
+
+1. Plan every participant independently with the existing RMF `Planner` to
+   establish feasibility, shortest-plan cost, and an initial conflict graph.
+2. If the independent plans are already mutually compatible, return them after
+   validating against the external schedule.
+3. Otherwise perform best-first search over priority-order prefixes. Extending
+   a node chooses one unplanned participant and replans only that participant
+   against external schedule traffic plus the already committed prefix plans.
+4. Validate each complete proposal pairwise, including initial and persistent
+   terminal occupancy, before accepting it.
+5. Rank complete/partial states by feasibility, makespan lower bound, total
+   arrival time, total delay from independent plans, and aggregate plan cost.
+   Waiting and detours are therefore penalized without favoring any particular
+   holding waypoint.
+
+Holding/yield routes are discovered by the existing Planner and graph metadata;
+the resolver does not manufacture a corridor resource. If all bounded priority
+orders fail, or any node/replan/time/participant budget is exceeded, the result
+is non-success and the caller falls back immediately to existing negotiation.
+
+## Route Validation and Schedule Semantics
+
+The local low-level validator ignores stale schedule itineraries for all
+participants inside the local conflict set, continues to validate against every
+external participant in the schedule snapshot, and additionally validates
+against prefix plans committed by the high-level search.
+
+Prefix reservations model trajectory conflicts plus stationary initial and
+terminal occupancy. Synthetic local reservations must not leak invalid schedule
+dependencies into returned plans; local planning disables dependency generation
+for those reservations. Final proposals are independently conflict-checked.
+External blockers remain reported to the caller.
+
+## CentralizedNegotiation Integration
+
+Add an opt-in `local_conflict_resolution(Options)` setting. When enabled,
+`solve()` invokes `LocalConflictResolver` before creating negotiation tables:
+
+- success: return the local proposal in the existing `Result::proposal()` type;
+- unsupported, infeasible, interrupted, or budget exceeded: run the original
+  negotiation implementation unchanged;
+- disabled (default): preserve baseline behavior exactly.
+
+Local metrics and whether fallback occurred are exposed for A/B diagnostics.
+This integration point is explicitly limited to core feasibility testing.
+
+## Verification
+
+Use the generic, topology-generated single-lane fixture without fixed IDs or
+robot names. Test 1-vs-1, dynamic same-direction third participant, dynamic
+opposite-direction third participant, 2-vs-1, 1-vs-2, and 2-vs-2. Dynamic cases
+are represented by replanning from the participants' actual progressed starts
+and the schedule state at injection time.
+
+For baseline and local modes record solve time, timeout, Planner calls or local
+replans, negotiation table count, rollout alternatives, local expanded nodes,
+completion, collision/deadlock, holding use, makespan, and total delay. The key
+target is to complete the previously 30-second-timeout dynamic-opposite and
+2-vs-2 cases collision-free and without face-to-face deadlock.
+
+Run focused resolver tests, the generic A/B suite, existing holding/rollout
+diagnostics, and the complete `rmf_traffic` regression suite. Store machine-
+readable and human-readable A/B results under the repository test results
+directory.
+
+## Non-goals and Limits
+
+Prioritized planning is not complete MAPF: a valid solution may exist outside
+the explored orderings or require explicit space-time constraints that the
+current Planner interface cannot express. Such cases must fall back rather than
+be reported as impossible. The PoC does not provide production distributed
+state gathering, task ownership, cancellation, or adapter-side execution.
